From 631d4d2e3cc5b5a755e2ab522d03b31f853dc544 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:49:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/28] flashnext: a shared round prices itself from the
 environment, and a draft row prices apart

Nothing is timed at load on the Flash Next CUDA backend, so a shared round is unpriced:
every draft the head's confidence rule lets through is verified. TF_FLASHNEXT_ROUND_MS
names the forward ms by rows (an "a+b" line, or a comma list from rows 1 that the core
interpolates between and extends past), so windows.allocate trims a round's drafts to the
rows that pay and observeOverhead measures each round's time past the forward as its
overhead. TF_FLASHNEXT_DRAFT_ROW_MS prices a draft row apart from the streams' rows (a
stream's further rows share its experts and cost less): the forward prices as the table at
the streams plus the draft row's ms for every further row, and the depth rule's
observeOverhead prices the round the same way. Unset, rounds stay unpriced as before and
no reply changes. Both settings have rows in platform/environment.zig: the round-ms table
is a .text setting whose reader owns the grammar (the validator refuses control
characters), the draft row's ms a real read through the table. The two shared-round tests
depth.zig carried move to depth_rounds_test.zig at the lean file cap. (#9999)
